00:00:02.360 --> 00:00:04.760 align:start position:0%
there's nothing wrong with getting a  little Sun it helps stimulate vitamin D  production and even helps us sleep  better but too much time catching some  rays can earn you a nasty sunburn and  increase your risk of harmful conditions  like skin cancer so how do you measure  how much UV exposure you're getting  people typically use wearable devices  known as dosimeter x' to track how large  of a dose they're getting of the sun's  ultraviolet rays but these devices have  relied on straps or clips attached to  clothing and rechargeable battery packs  making them cumbersome and susceptible  to water damage now researchers have  developed a wireless battery free  dosimeter that can stick directly to  skin or fingernails and measure exposure  to UV rays in real time the chip is tiny  the diameter around half the size of the  dimes it uses a photodiode a kind of  semiconductor that converts light into  electricity to not only track how much  light it's been exposed to but also to  power the device the tiny chip also  includes a sensor to monitor the skin's  temperature circuitry to create a  measurement of cumulative exposure and  an antenna to wirelessly send the  results to the users smartphone to test  the technology researchers had  volunteers wear multiple chips on  different parts of their bodies while  spending the day participating in a  variety of outdoor activities such as  hiking and swimming they also wore a  larger traditional dosimeter for  comparison while a few of the chips fell  off during the course of the experiment  the ones that didn't perform just as  well as the regular dosimeter x' and  showed different parts of the body  received different amounts of sunlight  with the upper body absorbing the lion's  share of ambient radiation the chips  could one day be used in medical  applications the researchers also tested  their chips on the chests of infants  receiving therapeutic blue light  treatments for neonatal jaundice using  the chips to precisely monitor each  patient's dose in real time could lead  to tailored and potentially more  effective light treatments  these little Sun chips could also be  used in worker safety tracking exposure  to UV rays for welders and steel workers  researchers envision a future where  these handy dosimeter x' would be part  of everyone's daily wardrobe stuck  directly onto clothing sunglasses or  skin let you know when it's time to stay  off the sunny side of the street  [Music]
 

